### What is inside a JWT and how is it validated?

A JWT has three base64-encoded parts separated by dots: a header (algorithm), a payload (claims), and a signature. The payload carries the subject, roles, and expiry. The signature is created with the authorization server's private key and verified by any service using the public key from a JWKS endpoint.

```java
package com.bank.security;

import io.jsonwebtoken.Claims;
import io.jsonwebtoken.Jwts;
import java.security.PublicKey;

public class JwtValidator {
    private final PublicKey publicKey;  // from JWKS endpoint

    public JwtValidator(PublicKey publicKey) {
        this.publicKey = publicKey;
    }

    public Claims validate(String token) {
        return Jwts.parserBuilder()
            .setSigningKey(publicKey)
            .build()
            .parseClaimsJws(token)
            .getBody();
    }
}
```

